meta {
  name: Onboard Agency
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/platform/agencies
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{platformAccessToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "agency": {
      "name": "PAC-69 Test Agency",
      "slug": "{{onboardSlug}}"
    },
    "branch": {
      "name": "Main",
      "address": {
        "street": "1 Main St",
        "city": "Oklahoma City",
        "state": "Oklahoma",
        "zip": "73102"
      }
    },
    "modules": ["dashboard", "leads", "clients", "performance", "mailers"],
    "owner": {
      "firstName": "Pac",
      "lastName": "Owner",
      "email": "{{onboardOwnerEmail}}"
    }
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
    const body = res.getBody();
    bru.setVar("onboardedAgencyId", body.agency.id);
    // Dev/test only — absent in production, where the email is the only
    // delivery channel. See the docs block.
    bru.setVar("onboardInviteToken", body.owner.inviteToken);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("creates the agency and its first branch", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.agency.slug).to.equal(bru.getVar("onboardSlug"));
    expect(body.branch.name).to.equal("Main");
  });

  test("invites the owner", function () {
    const owner = res.getBody().owner;
    expect(owner.email).to.equal(bru.getVar("onboardOwnerEmail"));
    // Absolute, and on the agency's own host — `HostTenantGuard` binds a
    // session to the hostname it was created on, so a link anywhere else is one
    // the owner cannot complete.
    expect(owner.inviteUrl).to.match(/^https?:\/\//);
    expect(owner.inviteUrl).to.contain("/auth/accept-invite?token=");
  });

  test("reports how the invite email went", function () {
    // `queued` normally. `failed` is still a 201 — see the docs block.
    expect(["queued", "failed"]).to.include(res.getBody().owner.emailStatus);
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /platform/agencies — onboard a whole tenant (PAC-69)

  Creates, in one call: the agency with the chosen module entitlements, its five
  default roles, its first branch, its post-sale audit checklist, and an invited
  **Agency Owner** who is emailed a link to set a password.

  - **Module:** none (`@SkipModule()`)
  - **Permission:** `platform:agencies:write`
  - **Data scope:** none — above the tenant boundary.

  ## What this replaced
  The route used to take `{ name, slug }` and create the agency document plus
  roles — no branch and no user, so nothing could be written to the tenant
  (`TenantRecord.branchId` is required) and nobody could sign in.

  ## Atomic in effect, with one deliberate exception
  A failure anywhere in the sequence rolls back every write, and re-submitting
  after a fix succeeds. The **invite email is outside that boundary**: it is
  dispatched after the tenant is committed and is never rolled back, because
  `InngestService.send` records the event before handing it over and the
  event-log sweep replays a stranded row — so undoing the tenant would mail a
  live link to a deleted account.

  A dispatch failure is therefore reported as `owner.emailStatus: "failed"` on a
  **201**, not an error. The tenant is correct; only the notification is
  missing. Recover with **Resend Owner Invite**.

  ## Body
  ```json
  {
    "agency": { "name": "Acme Insurance", "slug": "acme-insurance",
                "ticker": "ACM", "npn": "8675309",
                "carrierAppointments": [
                  { "carrierId": "<global carrier _id>",
                    "carrierAgencyCode": "A0B9049" }
                ] },
    "branch": { "name": "Main", "address": { "street": "…", "city": "…",
                "state": "…", "zip": "…" } },
    "modules": ["dashboard", "leads"],
    "owner": { "firstName": "Ada", "lastName": "Owner", "email": "ada@acme.test" }
  }
  ```

  `agency.ticker` and `agency.carrierAppointments` are optional but
  consequential (PAC-73, PAC-93): without the ticker the agency imports no
  mailers, and without an appointment every RTP upload warns because there is
  nothing to cross-check the file's `agencyid` against.

  **`carrierAgencyCode` is optional inside a row.** An operator who knows which
  carrier appointed the agency but not the code picks the carrier and moves on;
  a row with no code stores nothing, and the owner supplies it during their own
  setup or later under Workspace Settings. `carrierId` must be a **global**
  carrier — `GET /platform/carriers`, or `carriers` with `agencyId: null`. An
  agency-scoped custom carrier is a `400`.

  A `(carrierId, code)` pair belongs to one agency platform-wide; a second
  claim is a `409` naming the holder. Check one ahead of time with **Check
  Availability**. ⚠ Deactivating an appointment does *not* release its code —
  only removing it does.

  `branch.name` defaults to `Main` and its address is optional.
  `modules` is the enabled set — anything omitted is created disabled, not
  absent. `crm_service` is left out on purpose: **Accept Owner Invite** asserts
  that its permissions are filtered out of the owner's effective set, which is
  what proves entitlement gating actually reaches the resolver.

  ⚠ `mailers` is in the list for a **downstream** reason, not for this request.
  `Platform Mailer Campaigns` needs a *second* agency to prove PAC-71's
  one-lead-per-mailer rule — an agency that can see a shared campaign's mailer
  and is still refused when it tries to log a second lead from it. This is the
  only agency in the collection created after the demo tenant, and an agency
  with the module disabled would 403 there and prove nothing. Removing `mailers`
  here breaks those two requests, not this one.

  The slug must match `^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$`: it becomes the agency's
  subdomain the moment they add one.

  ## Response `201`
  ```json
  {
    "agency": { "id": "…", "name": "…", "slug": "…" },
    "branch": { "id": "…", "name": "Main" },
    "owner": {
      "userId": "…", "email": "…",
      "inviteUrl": "https://…/auth/accept-invite?token=…",
      "expiresAt": "2026-09-09T…Z",
      "emailStatus": "queued",
      "inviteToken": "…"
    }
  }
  ```

  `owner.inviteToken` is a **bearer credential** and is returned only outside
  production, so the flow can be walked locally where mail goes to a log.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — malformed slug, missing owner name, invalid email,
    unknown module key.
  - `409 Conflict` — the slug or ticker belongs to another agency, or the owner
    address already belongs to an account (the message distinguishes an existing
    member from a pending invite).
  - `403 Forbidden` — not a platform account.

  ## ⚠ Residue
  There is no delete-agency endpoint, so every `bru run` of this folder leaves
  one agency behind. The slug and owner email are timestamped by **Check
  Availability** so repeated runs do not collide.
}
